# Project: OSRS Private Server (2009scape) + Distributable Client

## What this project is
A private OSRS server (2009-era content) built on 2009scape, self-hosted on
your local server, with a pre-packaged client distributed to friends for
double-click-and-play access over the internet.

## Project root
D:\OSRS\2009Scape — IntelliJ project root, containing Server/ and Client/ as
subfolders. The wider D:\OSRS folder has other abandoned experiments (old
228 attempts) that live outside this project root and are not part of this
codebase.

## Repos / components
- Server/  — 2009scape server + management server
  Source: https://gitlab.com/2009scape/2009scape.git
  Build system: Maven — actual pom.xml lives at Server/Server (nested folder,
  not the Server/ root itself)
  Also contains Server/Tools/Frostys Cache Editor — a separate Eclipse
  project/tool, not part of the core server build, not a current priority
  A Management Server component is referenced in the repo description but
  its exact build location hasn't been confirmed yet
- Client/  — RT4 client (official 2009scape-maintained fork, not the older
  Pazaz/RT4-Client original)
  Source: https://gitlab.com/2009scape/rt4-client.git
  Build system: Gradle
- distribution/ — (not yet created) packaging scripts, bundled JRE, output
  zip for friend-facing distribution

## Git
Server/ and Client/ are two independent git repositories, each with their
own .git folder. The parent D:\OSRS\2009Scape folder is not itself a repo —
running git commands from that top level will correctly fail. Both should
be registered as separate VCS roots in IntelliJ (File → Settings → Version
Control) so branch/commit tracking works per-repo.

## Key decisions (don't relitigate these without discussion)
- We are NOT using the Saradomin launcher for distribution. It is hard-coded
  to only connect to the local computer and cannot point at a custom server
  IP/hostname.
- Instead: use the raw RT4 client directly, with a pre-edited config
  pointing at our server, bundled with a portable JRE 11, so friends can
  double-click and play without editing anything.
- Reference for packaging later: gitlab.com/2009scape/singleplayer/windows
  (crossplatform-release) — an official example of bundling client+server+JRE
  together for a similar "just run it" experience. Not something we build
  from directly, just a structural reference for the packaging step.

## Networking
- Server-facing hostname: danny-games.servegame.com (No-IP dynamic DNS)
- Game port: confirm actual port from Server's config before hardcoding
  anywhere — do not assume a value
- Router port forwarding is handled outside this repo

## Conventions & constraints
- Never hardcode a local/LAN IP anywhere in client or server config —
  always use the No-IP hostname, since distributed clients must work over
  the internet, not just localhost.
- Server = Maven, Client = Gradle — this is intentional, don't try to unify
  build tooling.
- Client config changes (server address/port) are deliberate and
  packaging-related — flag any change to this clearly.

## Things to avoid
- Don't reintroduce Saradomin launcher as the distribution method.
- Don't assume the game port without checking Server's actual config file.
- Don't touch anything in D:\OSRS outside the 2009Scape/ project folder.